  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nIssued at 202 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThis Weekend...\n\nA beautiful weekend is ahead of us. Warm front will have fully moved \nnortheast and out of the area, leaving us under decent warm \nadvection through the weekend. As such, temperatures are expected to \nincrease well above seasonal normals, especially Saturday with the \nstrongest warm advection. Widespread temperatures in the low 80s are \nexpected Saturday, with mid-upper 70s Sunday. Mostly clear \nconditions will also prevail, with some passing high clouds on \nSunday associated with cirrus from Isaias post-landfall. \n\nNext Week...\n\nZonal flow will start the new work week, with llvl flow increasing \nout of the southwest ahead of a passing weak shortwave. Monday will \nremain dry and unseasonably mild, with the wave and associated cold \nfront passing through Monday night into Tuesday. Moisture will \nremain limited ahead of the front, which should limit \nprecipitation chances for most (<15% chances). Thus, a largely \ndry forecast. After that wave, we will see brief ridging prior \nto a strong wave deepening over the Rockies. As this deepens, \nsouthwest flow will set up over the Central CONUS, shunting the \nridge east Wednesday. Warm advection will increase overhead \nthrough midweek, with shortwaves passing through ahead of this \ndeep wave. Thus, a more active pattern will take shape and \nchances for showers/storms will be on the rise midweek and \nbeyond. It is too soon to determine the threat of strong/severe \nstorms, along with heavy rain potential.",
